{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And he saw the first part for himself.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it says that Gad sought for himself the land of Sihon and Og, which was the beginning of the conquest of the land, for there the portion of the lawgiver (מחוקק) is the great one and the ruler, as in (Judges 5:9): ״לבי לחוקקי ישראל״. And ספון means 'covered,' as in (Jeremiah 22:14): ״וספון בארז״, and likewise (Haggai 1:4): ״לשבת בבתיכם ספונים״. And he said ספון and not קבור ('buried') to hint that he is stored away for the resurrection of the dead, and therefore he was buried in the portion of Gad, outside the land, so that the dead of outside the land might be revived through his merit." "<b>And he came with the heads of the people.</b> That the heads of the tribe of Gad came with Israel as vanguard troops, for so they stipulated with Israel." "<b>The righteousness of YHWH he performed.</b> That he fulfilled the utterance of his lips, as he said (Numbers 32:18): ״לא נשוב אל בתינו עד התנחל״ and so forth." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ״וירא ראשית לו״ — the world was created in the merit of Moses, who is called ראשית ('first'), as it is said: ״וירא ראשית לו כי שם חלקת מחוקק ספון״." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> ״וירא ראשית לו״ — ״וירא״ (and He saw) — ״מרחיב גד ראשית לו״ — the Gad who expands, the first part is His. The matter is that the Holy One, blessed be He, looked into Wisdom (חכמה), which is ראשית, in order to create the world, for there is the attribute of ראשית in the beings (הויות) that were within Wisdom. He saw that the portion of Moses, who is called מחוקק ('lawgiver'), is ספון ('covered') and hidden there, for he is included within the ראשית. ״ויתא ראשי עם״ — from there He emanated the attributes (מדות), which He called ראשי עם ('heads of the people'), in the language of (Psalms 24:7): ״שאו שערים ראשיכם״. ״צדקת ה' עשה״ and ״ומשפטיו״ — these are תפארת and כבוד, which were emanated from there, and this is clear." "<b>And the matter of</b> the Midrash and its secret concerning Moses, that he is called ראשית (\"beginning\") because his prophecy was through the clarifying glass (aspaklaria ha-me'irah), which is ראשית, for only to that point did he grasp and from there he prophesied. For every prophet prophesies from within what he has grasped; and even though he prophesied from within it, he did not grasp its essence — and on this he asked, saying (Exodus 33:18): ״הראני נא את כבודך״, and he was not answered. And the reason he is called מחוקק (lawgiver) is that the prophecy of Moses was in the Great Name, in the attribute of mercy, from which come the positive commandments, which are 248 (רמ״ח), for the positive and negative commandments correspond to the Tiferet and the Hod; therefore the number of positive commandments is inscribed in the word מחקק, for they correspond to the Special Name, and the number of negative commandments, 365 (שס״ה), which correspond to אל״ף דל״ת, is likewise alluded to in the verse (Daniel 9:19): ״אדני ש׳מעה אדני ס׳לחה אדני ה׳קשיבה״. And it appears that Onkelos alluded to this when he translated ספרא רבא — calling the lawgiver \"the Great\" — because his prophecy was in the Great Name. And Isaiah the prophet, of blessed memory, mentioned (Isaiah 33:22): ״כי ה׳ שופטנו ה׳ מחוקקנו ה׳ מלכנו הוא יושיענו״, and the explanation of the verse you will find stated explicitly and in order from below upward. And even though Rashi, of blessed memory, interpreted מחוקקנו in the sense of ruler, from the expression (Genesis 49:10) ״ומחוקק מבין רגליו״, what I have alluded to is the principal meaning. And Jonathan ben Uzziel, of blessed memory, testifies to this, for he interpreted מחוקקנו as \"he gave us a religion/law\" — this is what he translated: ה׳ דייננא דאפקנא בגבורתיה ממצרים מלפננא די יהב לנא אולפן אורייתא מסיני. And so it is the truth, for the Torah was given in the Great Name." "<b>And from here</b> you should be roused to ask concerning the sacred writings, regarding those things that are called by the name גדול (\"great\"), as in the case of the Temple, which is called גדול, as it says (II Kings 25:9): ״ואת כל בית גדול שרף באש״; and likewise the officiating minister there is called (Numbers 35:25): ״הכהן הגדול״; and likewise the river in the land of Israel is called (Deuteronomy 1:7): ״הנהר הגדול נהר פרת״; and likewise the Great Name before which the High priest officiates, as it says (Deut. 11 — i.e., Deuteronomy 10:17): ״את כל מעשה ה׳ הגדול״. And likewise in that which the masters of truth call the expression רבא, which is the Aramaic translation of גדול, as in the Kaddish: אמן יהא שמיה רבא מברך; and likewise יהא שלמא רבא; and likewise Kedushah of the day, which they call קדושא רבא, and not so that of the night; and likewise the blessing of the day, אהבת עולם אהבתנו, which they call אהבה רבה, and not so that of the evening prayer; and likewise they call the seventh day of the willow הושענא רבא, because it is the completion of 26 days from the creation of the world, which was created on the 25th of Elul. All these, if you contemplate them, you will find that one path is common to them all. And understand this."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {5 "Daniel 9:19 citation is accurate. The apostrophes in ש׳מעה, ס׳לחה, ה׳קשיבה mark the consonants ש, ס, ה in the printed Hebrew, apparently marking letters for some count or emphasis; reproduced as in the source.", 6 "The phrase את כל מעשה ה׳ הגדול does not appear verbatim in Deuteronomy; the nearest text is Deuteronomy 10:17 (ה׳ אלהיכם אלהי האלהים ואדני האדנים האל הגדול). The printed source writes (שם י״א) i.e. ibid. verse 11, apparently a citation error for 10:17. Translated hyper-literally as printed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:42.992836023Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}